Pattern matching for instanceof.

For decades, testing an object's type in Java meant an awkward three-step dance. Check with instanceof, then cast to that type, then use it. The check and the cast repeated the type name, and the cast could drift out of sync with the check. Pattern matching for instanceof, which has been final since Java sixteen, folds all three steps into one.

Look at the old ceremony. You test whether obj is a string; then, inside the if, you cast obj to string and assign it to s; then you use s. The type name string is written twice, and nothing forces the cast to match the test. The new form replaces all of that. You write, if obj is an instance of string s. That "string s" is a type pattern. If obj is a string, the test is true, and s is bound to it, already cast — no separate cast line, no repeated type, and no chance of the two disagreeing. It reads simply as "if obj is a string, call it s."

The clever part is where s exists. It's in scope exactly where the compiler can prove the match succeeded. This is called flow scoping, and it follows the logic of your code. Consider: if obj is an instance of string s, and s dot length is greater than three. Here s is in scope after the and, because an and only evaluates its right side when the left side was true. Or consider an early return: if obj is not an instance of string s, then return. After that if, on the next line, s is actually in scope — because the only way to reach that line is if the match succeeded. But if you write obj is an instance of string s, or s is empty, that won't compile, because after an or, s is not guaranteed to be bound. The binding is available precisely when it's safe, and nowhere it isn't, and the compiler tracks that for you. That and case, in particular, is why a type test followed by an and, and then a use of the binding, is completely safe.

Why does this matter, beyond the tidiness? Removing the cast removes a real class of bug, because the check and the cast can no longer specify different types. But the deeper reason it's here is that this same type pattern is the building block for the far more powerful pattern matching in switch, coming next, and for record patterns that destructure data, in section nine. Learning it first in the small instanceof case means the switch version will feel like nothing new.

One note on where to reach for it. Pattern matching makes type tests clean, but it doesn't make them good design. A method built from a long ladder of "if x is an instance of A, else if x is an instance of B" is often begging to be a polymorphic method, from module two, or an exhaustive switch over a sealed type. So use instanceof patterns for genuine boundaries — parsing untyped input, interop, an equals implementation — and prefer dispatch, or a sealed switch, when you're branching over a family that you control. And that's exactly the case the next section handles.
